---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import GazaStatusVisual from "../../components/GazaStatusVisual.astro";
import ProbabilityStrip from "../../components/ProbabilityStrip.astro";
import { pageIntroductions } from "../../lib/introductions.js";
import { gazaStatus, unpublishedArms, controlledStatements, engineById, urls, jewishIsraelFindings } from "../../lib/site.js";
import { fmt, signed, size } from "../../scripts/util.js";

const intro = pageIntroductions.antisemitismIsrael;
const F = jewishIsraelFindings();
const name = (e) => engineById[e].label;
const range = (lo, hi) => `95% range ${fmt(lo)} to ${fmt(hi)}`;
---
<Base title={intro.title} description={intro.intro} path={`${urls.home()}antisemitism-and-israel/`} current={{ page: "antisemitism-israel" }}
  crumbs={[{ label: "Leaderboard", href: urls.home() }, { label: "Antisemitism, Israeli identity and anti-Israel claims" }]}>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <p class="kicker">Related studies</p>
    <h1 class="display xl">{intro.title}</h1>
    <p class="deck">{intro.intro}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" id="findings" aria-labelledby="findings-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="findings-h">What we found</h2>
    <p class="lede">Four kinds of test. When a text says a person is Jewish, all three models lean slightly toward antisemitic stereotypes. When a loan application says its principal is Israeli, the models differ by a point or two from their average for all thirteen nationalities, in both directions, and each treats some other nationality least favourably.</p>
    <ol class="findings">
      <li>
        <h3>Antisemitic stereotypes: a small, consistent lean in every model</h3>
        <p>We add a phrase saying a person is Jewish and ask yes-or-no questions built on six documented stereotypes. The score is how much further that phrase pushes the model toward the stereotyped answer than the same phrase for Christian or Muslim people does.</p>
        <ul>
          {F.bios.map((b) => {
            const l = F.loans.find((x) => x.engine === b.engine);
            return <li><strong>{name(b.engine)}</strong> leans toward the stereotype on {b.clear} of {b.of} stereotypes in biographies, most on {b.label} (+{size(b.value)} points, {range(b.lo, b.hi)}){l ? <>; in loan narratives, most on {l.label} (+{size(l.value)} points)</> : null}.</li>;
          })}
        </ul>
        <p class="note">These are shifts of a few percentage points in the model's answers. <a href={`${urls.home()}antisemitism/`}>Every stereotype, wording and model</a>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Occupation decisions: the answer moves, with no clear direction of harm</h3>
        <p>Saying a biography's subject is Jewish moves which of two occupations the model picks.</p>
        <ul>
          {F.decisions.map((b) => <li><strong>{name(b.engine)}</strong> moves by up to {size(b.value)} points, most on {b.label} ({range(b.lo, b.hi)}), with a clear effect on {b.clear} of {b.of} decisions.</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="note">The choice is between two occupations such as surgeon and physician, so the move shows the model reacts to the phrase, not that it treats the person worse. <a href={urls.dim("antisemitism-decisions")}>Each decision</a>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Israeli nationality in lending: small differences, in both directions</h3>
        <p>The same loan applications name the principal's nationality, one of thirteen. Compared with the average of all thirteen on the same applications:</p>
        <ul>
          {F.israeli.map((r) => <li><strong>{name(r.engine)}</strong> is {r.vsMean.mean_pts >= 0 ? "more" : "less"} confident in approving an Israeli principal by {size(Math.abs(r.vsMean.mean_pts))} points ({range(r.vsMean.ci_pts[0], r.vsMean.ci_pts[1])}).</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="note">Against the "keen cyclist" control, every nationality, Israeli included, lowers approval confidence, so that comparison mostly shows the effect of naming any nationality. The least favoured nationality is {F.palestinianLowest.map((r, i) => <>{i ? "; " : ""}{r.nationality.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + r.nationality.slice(1)} for {name(r.engine)} ({signed(r.value)})</>)}. <a href={`${urls.dim("nationality")}#nationality-lending-relative`}>All thirteen nationalities</a>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Direct statements: {F.claims.length ? "tested with controls" : "not yet tested with controls"}</h3>
        {F.claims.length
          ? <ul>{F.claims.map((c) => <li>{c.rejects === c.n
  ? <><strong>{name(c.engine)}</strong> rejects all {c.n} claims (antisemitic, anti-Israel, Holocaust-distortion, and matched conspiracy claims about no group) and agrees with each one's reversal.</>
  : c.shown === 0
    ? <><strong>{name(c.engine)}</strong> shows no consistent position on any of the {c.n} claims{c.order >= 0.5 ? ": it mostly picks whichever answer is listed first" : c.nearEven ? ": its chance of agreeing stays close to even for both a claim and its reversal" : ""}.</>
    : <><strong>{name(c.engine)}</strong> shows a consistent position on {c.shown} of {c.n} claims, rejecting {c.rejects}.</>}</li>)}</ul>
          : null}
        <p class="note">Each statement is paired with a written reversal and asked in both option orders, so a model that agrees with everything is not mistaken for one that agrees with the claim. {["jev", "laya", "kev"].filter((e) => !F.claims.some((c) => c.engine === e)).map(name).join(" and ")} {F.claims.length < 3 ? "have not answered this version yet." : ""} <a href="#direct-statements">The statements and answers</a>.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="start-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="start-h">Start with the question you have</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <article class="result-card"><h3>Does a model repeat antisemitic stereotypes?</h3><p>Read the dedicated study across professional biographies and synthetic loan narratives. It tests model answers to stereotype questions, not people.</p><a href={`${urls.home()}antisemitism/`}>Open antisemitic stereotype results</a></article>
      <article class="result-card"><h3>Does stating Israeli nationality change a decision?</h3><p>Compare the same small-business-loan application after a nationality sentence changes. This is treatment in a loan decision, not a test of political views.</p><a href={urls.dim("nationality")}>Open nationality decision results</a></article>
      <article class="result-card"><h3>How do nationality wordings move loan-narrative answers?</h3><p>Inspect the full per-question record for the same thirteen nationalities in 200 synthetic loan narratives. It is reported as raw model response shifts, not a cross-nationality verdict.</p><a href="https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/studies/loan-narratives-antisemitism-nationality-loan-axis.jsonl">Open the saved narrative-study result</a></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-split" aria-labelledby="map-h">
    <div class="sec-head">
    <h2 class="display" id="map-h">What each study does—and does not—measure</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sec-body">
    <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Map of antisemitism and Israel-related studies">
      <table class="cell-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Study</th><th scope="col">What changes or is asked</th><th scope="col">What it does not establish</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><a href={`${urls.home()}antisemitism/`}>Antisemitic stereotype study</a></th><td>A biography or loan narrative says a person is Jewish, devout Jewish, Israeli, connected with a synagogue, or has a Jewish-associated surname.</td><td>It does not measure a model’s views on the Israeli–Palestinian conflict or the truth of any stereotype.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><a href={urls.dim("nationality")}>Israeli-nationality decision arm</a></th><td>The same synthetic loan application identifies its principal with a nationality, including Israeli and Palestinian.</td><td>It does not measure beliefs about Israel, Palestine, Zionism, a government, or a conflict.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
    </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {gazaStatus.results.length > 0 && <section class="wrap section sec-split" id="gaza-israel-hamas" aria-labelledby="gaza-h">
    <div class="sec-head">
    <h2 class="display" id="gaza-h">Gaza/Israel–Hamas direct-response study</h2>
    <p class="lede">Laya, Jev and Kev each directly assessed all 15 statements ADL categorized as “War in Gaza/Israel and Hamas.” Each case uses a structured <q>Decision requested for</q> field, either with no name supplied or one of six names. The tables report each model’s responses, not a conclusion about people or a reproduction of ADL’s multi-model findings.</p>
    <p class="note"><strong>Direct question:</strong> <q>{gazaStatus.question}</q> The available responses are strongly agree, somewhat agree, somewhat disagree, and strongly disagree.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sec-body">
    <GazaStatusVisual study={gazaStatus} />
    <div class="probability-legend" aria-label="Probability strip legend"><span><i class="s1"></i>Strongly agree</span><span><i class="s2"></i>Somewhat agree</span><span><i class="s3"></i>Somewhat disagree</span><span><i class="s4"></i>Strongly disagree</span></div>
    {gazaStatus.results.map((result) => <>
      <h3 class="display sm">{result.label}: no-name control</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label={`${result.label} no-name Gaza Israel Hamas responses`}>
        <table class="cell-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">exact statement</th><th scope="col">selected response</th><th scope="col">response probabilities</th><th scope="col">mean response</th></tr></thead><tbody>
          {gazaStatus.statements.map((statement) => { const answer = result.statements[statement.id].statuses.no_name; return <tr><th scope="row"><q>{statement.text}</q></th><td><strong>{answer.clear_choice ? answer.choice : "no clear choice"}</strong></td><td><ProbabilityStrip options={gazaStatus.options} probabilities={answer.probabilities} label={`${result.label}, ${statement.text}, no name`} /></td><td>{answer.mean_response.toFixed(4)}</td></tr>; })}
        </tbody></table>
      </div>
      <p class="note">Mean response is the recorded probability-weighted four-response scale. It keeps all choices visible; it is not a bias score.</p>
      <details class="claim-detail"><summary><strong class="opinion-claim-family">All decision-request status values</strong><span class="opinion-claim-summary">15 statements × a no-name control and six named fields</span></summary>
        <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label={`${result.label} Gaza Israel Hamas decision statuses`}>
          <table class="cell-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">statement</th>{Object.entries(gazaStatus.statuses).map(([status, value]) => <th scope="col">{status === "no_name" ? "no name" : value.replace(".", "")}</th>)}</tr></thead><tbody>
            {gazaStatus.statements.map((statement) => <tr><th scope="row"><q>{statement.text}</q></th>{Object.keys(gazaStatus.statuses).map((status) => { const answer = result.statements[statement.id].statuses[status]; const request = status === "no_name" ? "no name" : gazaStatus.statuses[status]; return <td><strong>{answer.clear_choice ? answer.choice : "no clear choice"}</strong><ProbabilityStrip options={gazaStatus.options} probabilities={answer.probabilities} label={`${result.label}, ${statement.text}, ${request}`} /></td>; })}</tr>)}
          </tbody></table>
        </div>
      </details>
    </>)}
    <p class="note">Read the <a href="https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/docs/gaza-israel-hamas-decision-status-preregistration.md">study plan written before the prompts were run</a>, the <a href="https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/studies/gaza-israel-hamas-decision-status.jsonl">scored study row</a>, and the saved raw answers for <a href="https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/answers/laya/gaza-israel-hamas-decision-status/as-written.jsonl.gz">Laya</a>, <a href="https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/answers/jev/gaza-israel-hamas-decision-status/as-written.jsonl.gz">Jev</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/answers/kev/gaza-israel-hamas-decision-status/as-written.jsonl.gz">Kev</a>. The source framework is ADL’s <a href="https://www.adl.org/resources/report/generating-hate-anti-jewish-and-anti-israel-bias-leading-large-language-models">Generating Hate</a> and <a href="https://www.adl.org/adl-ai-index">AI Index</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>}

  {controlledStatements.arms.length > 0 && <section class="wrap section sec-split" id="direct-statements" aria-labelledby="ds-h">
    <div class="sec-head">
    <h2 class="display" id="ds-h">Direct statements, with controls</h2>
    <p class="lede">We showed the model each statement and a written reversal of it, each in two option orders, and asked whether it agrees. A model that agrees with a statement and with its reversal is not taking a position, so a position is shown only where the model agrees with one and not the other in both orders (a difference of at least {Math.round(controlledStatements.rule.min_stance * 100)} points, with agreement to both, or to neither, within {Math.round(controlledStatements.rule.max_acquiescence * 100)} points of what a consistent reader would give). Otherwise the row says the model shows no consistent position.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sec-body">
    {controlledStatements.arms.map((arm) => {
      const en = engineById[arm.engine];
      const pct = (v) => `${Math.round(v * 100)}%`;
      const shown = arm.pairs.filter((p) => p.shown).length;
      const order = arm.pairs.reduce((t, p) => t + p.order_effect, 0) / arm.pairs.length;
      return (
        <div class="ds-arm">
          <h3>{arm.label}: {en.label}</h3>
          <p class="note">{en.label} shows a consistent position on {shown} of {arm.pairs.length} pairs. The percentages give how likely {en.label} is to agree, averaged over the two option orders.{order >= 0.5 ? ` ${en.label} mostly picks whichever answer is listed first: on average, moving an answer to the front raises its chance of being picked by ${Math.round(order * 100)} points, so its answers here say more about the order of the options than about the statement.` : arm.pairs.every((p) => Math.abs(p.agree_s - 0.5) < 0.25 && Math.abs(p.agree_r - 0.5) < 0.35) ? ` ${en.label}'s chance of agreeing stays close to even for both a statement and its reversal, so it does not take a side.` : ""}</p>
          <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label={`${arm.label}: ${en.label}`}>
            <table>
              <caption class="sr">{arm.label}: {en.label}</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Statement</th><th scope="col">Agrees</th><th scope="col">Reversal</th><th scope="col">Agrees</th><th scope="col">{en.label}'s position</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {arm.pairs.map((p) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{p.statement}</td><td>{pct(p.agree_s)}</td>
                    <td>{p.reversal}</td><td>{pct(p.agree_r)}</td>
                    <td>{!p.shown ? "No consistent position" : p.stance > 0 ? "Agrees with the statement" : "Agrees with the reversal"}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="note">Read the <a href={`https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/${controlledStatements.plan}`}>plan written before any answer</a>, the <a href={`https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/${arm.study}`}>scored study row</a> and the <a href={`https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/${arm.record}`}>saved answers</a> ({arm.model}). Other models have not answered this version yet.</p>
        </div>
      );
    })}
    </div>
  </section>}

  <section class="wrap section sec-split" id="not-shown-yet" aria-labelledby="unpub-h">
    <div class="sec-head">
    <h2 class="display" id="unpub-h">Not shown yet</h2>
    <p class="lede">The first two arms that tested direct statements are not shown: each statement was asked once, in one wording and one option order, and the models agreed with statements that contradict each other. The controlled version above replaces them. Their saved answers and study plans stay in the repository.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sec-body">
    {unpublishedArms.map((arm) => (
      <details class="claim-detail">
        <summary><strong>{arm.label}</strong></summary>
        <p><strong>Why not shown:</strong> {arm.reason}</p>
        <p><strong>Returns when:</strong> {arm.returns_when}</p>
        <p><strong>Study file:</strong> <a href={`https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/${arm.study}`}>{arm.study}</a></p>
        <p><strong>Saved answers:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          {arm.records.map((record) => (
            <li><a href={`https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/${record}`}>{record}</a></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </details>
    ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="sources-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="sources-h">Source and study records</h2>
    <p class="lede">See the <a href="https://www.adl.org/adl-ai-index">ADL AI Index</a>, the <a href="https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/docs/antisemitic-tropes-preregistration.md">stereotype study plan</a>, and the <a href="https://github.com/AnthusAI/Biased-Decisions/blob/main/docs/tropes-round2-sources.md">ADL source review</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
